## TIER: TIER 1, argued (the drafter's reading; Tuesday confirms at stamp)
**Tiers:** full weight (TIER 1) for security surfaces, data destruction, deploys, customer-facing shipped artefacts and human handovers; through-code only for tests/docs/config/already-gated follow-ups.
**The case FOR through-code:** the diff is small (+350/−28, five static JS files, one test, counts); each hunk is an escape at an interpolation or a DOM construction; red-first jsdom cells exist; CodeQL on the PR head reports nothing.
**Why that loses: TIER 1.** This is a **security surface** (8 high-severity CodeQL alerts, rule `js/xss-through-dom`, open on main at `c7ca0ed`), and Tuesday's ANSWER-12 rules it so: *"QA tier 1: security, browser-rendered. Its gate will drive a real browser to compare renders."* The builder's own evidence is jsdom only (READY NOT TESTED: *"A real browser render. Your gate's browser leg compares renders; mine is jsdom."*), the cells run functions LIFTED from the files by a regex (WRONG 6), and the render-identity claim is the builder's script against the builder's inputs. "Inert" and "unchanged" are properties of the page as a browser runs it. **The verdict is PROVISIONAL if the SITE row (r2), the RED-PROOF row (r3), the BROWSER-INERT row (b1) or the BROWSER-RENDER row (b2) did not run.**

